d.evidence:
1) Weight statistics and printed first-layer 3x3 filters: mostly positive-bias, negative-sum background units plus a few negative-bias bright colour edge detectors.
2) Uniform images from -3 to 255: logits are enormous away from 0 to 1. Pure black gives edge-on with bulge at +39 and spiral classes at -20 to -55. Sensible small logits appear only near 0.1 to 1, so I assumed 0 to 1 input with sky around 0.1.
3) Sweeping the sky level from 0.07 to 0.3 with 10 synthetic galaxies: below 0.08 everything goes to edge-on with bulge. From 0.09 to 0.12 predictions are sensible. At 0.15 or more, spirals and edge-ons flip to disturbed or merging.
4) At sky 0.11:
- an axis-ratio sweep (q 1 to 0.05) traced round, then in-between, then edge-on;
- the orientation sweep was invariant;
- the bulge-size sweep flipped edge-on without bulge to edge-on with bulge at radius 2 or more;
- size and amplitude sweeps strengthened round with size;
- off-centre position weakened round;
- pure single-colour blobs lost the round call.
5) Spiral pitch, arm count and bar sweeps gave tight, loose and barred as described. Pair separation sweeps and a bridge gave a weak merging vote.
6) Pooled-channel differences against empty sky for each probe type, and per-cell class evidence maps: evidence sits on the central galaxy cells.
